      "Formulated by the manufacturer for kitchen and bath areas with mould-resistant properties",
      "Available in several colours to match a dark or light slab",
      "Easy to apply with a standard caulk gun"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Silicone cannot be painted and is difficult to remove cleanly, so apply with care",
      "Seams and structural joints are a fabricator job, not a DIY caulk job"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right sealant for the wall and sink-edge joints that move. It is a finishing product, not a way to fix a slab seam.",
    "description": "A countertop is only as watertight as its joints. Where the slab meets the wall, the backsplash or the sink, a flexible sealant takes up the small movements that a rigid material cannot. GE Silicone 2 Kitchen & Bath is a widely used choice, and the manufacturer designs it for those wet areas with mould-resistant properties. The trade-off is permanence: silicone does not take paint and is awkward to remove, so it rewards patience on the first application. Tape the joint, tool a clean bead and remove the tape before the skin forms. Do not use it to fill a gap between two slabs or to repair a chip. Those are structural and cosmetic tasks that fabricators handle with colour-matched epoxy adhesives. For a gun that makes a neat bead easier, see our guide to caulk guns."
