      "Small hole sizes cover soap dispensers and faucet holes",
      "Far cheaper than a failed slab, if used by someone experienced"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slab cutouts carry real chipping and cracking risk and are best left to a fabricator",
      "Needs a steady wet-drilling technique and eye, hearing and respiratory protection"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A tool for the confident DIYer drilling a small, planned hole. For sink and hob cutouts, hire the fabricator.",
    "description": "Drilling sintered stone is not like drilling granite or laminate. The surface is extremely hard, brittle at the edges and unforgiving of heat. A diamond core bit is the correct tool type, because the diamond grit grinds rather than cuts and the hollow centre keeps the cut cool when run with a steady trickle of water. SHDIATOOL is a brand that sells diamond bits aimed at porcelain tile, which is the closest widely sold relative. Treat this as a tool for small, planned holes such as a soap dispenser, and only after practising on off-cut porcelain. Wet cutting also keeps silica dust down, which matters because cutting any silica-bearing stone makes respirable dust. Large cutouts, corner radii and sink fitting are fabrication work. Ask the fabricator to do them in the shop."
  }
