          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-sintered-stone-countertops/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is sintered stone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sintered stone is a manufactured surface made from natural mineral powders that are compressed under high pressure and fired at high temperature until they fuse. It contains no resin binder, which is why it is non-porous and resistant to heat and sunlight. Dekton, Neolith and Lapitec are well-known brands."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is sintered stone better than quartz?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on your priorities. Sintered stone resists heat and ultraviolet light better because it has no resin, and it needs no sealing. Quartz is generally easier to fabricate and more forgiving of knocks. Many buyers choose sintered stone for outdoor kitchens and cooking areas, and quartz for general indoor use."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sintered stone stain or scratch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers market it as highly resistant to both, and because the surface is non-porous, liquids sit on top rather than soaking in. It is very hard, but hardness makes edges brittle. Chipping at corners is a more realistic risk than scratching, so choose a protective edge profile."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you put hot pans on sintered stone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers rate their slabs for contact with hot cookware, which is a key advantage over quartz. Sudden extreme temperature changes can still stress any mineral surface, especially near edges and cutouts, so many fabricators suggest a trivet for very hot pans. Check your specific range for the stated limits."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sintered stone need sealing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally no. Because it is non-porous, a sealer has nothing to penetrate and can leave a film. Most major brands state that sealing is unnecessary. Check the care instructions for your specific slab, and follow the brand guidance if it differs from general advice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What thickness of sintered stone should I choose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Slabs are commonly offered at around 12mm and 20mm. The 12mm option is lighter and usually needs continuous support underneath, while 20mm can span cabinets more independently. Follow the manufacturer span and overhang limits, and ask your fabricator which suits your layout."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I cut or drill sintered stone myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Small, planned holes are possible with a diamond core bit, water cooling and practice on offcuts. Large cutouts, sink openings and edges should be done by a fabricator. Cutting any silica-bearing stone makes respirable dust, so use water, a respirator and eye protection."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
