        <div id="what-is-sintered-stone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Sintered Stone Actually Is (and What It Is Not)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sintered stone is a manufactured surface made from natural mineral powders, typically quartz, feldspar, clays and mineral oxides, that are compressed under very high pressure and then fired at high temperature until the particles fuse. That firing step is the point of the name: "sintering" means bonding powder into a solid without melting it completely. The result is a dense, non-porous slab with no resin binder holding it together. Brands such as Dekton, Neolith and Lapitec are the best-known names, and each has its own recipe and finish range.
          </p>

          <p>
            That recipe is why the material behaves differently from the countertops most people know. Quartz countertops are mostly crushed quartz held together with polymer resin. Granite is a quarried natural stone with pores and veins. Sintered stone is closer in spirit to a very large, very hard ceramic. Because there is no resin and almost no porosity, manufacturers promote its resistance to heat, ultraviolet light, staining and scratching. Those are marketing claims worth taking seriously, but also worth reading carefully, as later sections show.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth being clear about what this page is. Sintered slabs are not an Amazon purchase. They are sold through stone suppliers and fabricators, cut to your template in a workshop and installed by a crew with the right equipment. What you can buy yourself are the supporting products: cleaners suited to the surface, a sealant for the joints that move, and, for the experienced, a diamond bit for small holes. Those are the seven picks below, and we have been honest about where each one fits and where it does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sintered stone also overlaps with a few terms you will see in showrooms: "ultra-compact surface", "porcelain slab" and "large-format porcelain". The first is a trade description, and the last two usually refer to related products with similar manufacturing. The practical advice is the same for all of them. Ask the supplier for the technical data sheet, ask what thickness and finish you are being quoted for, and ask which cleaners the manufacturer approves. That one conversation answers most of the questions that come up later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 7 Best Products for Sintered Stone Countertops
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sintered stone slabs are bought through stone suppliers and fabricators, not online, so these seven picks are the supporting products you can buy yourself: cleaners, a sealant for the joints that move, and a diamond bit for small holes. We chose them for how well they suit a non-porous surface and say plainly where each one is a poor fit. The links below are affiliate links, which means we may earn a commission if you buy through them, at no extra cost to you. We have not run lab tests on these products, so the descriptions rely on what the manufacturers state and what owners commonly report.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
