        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sintered stone is a genuinely good countertop for the right household. It shrugs off heat and sunlight, never needs sealing and cleans with a damp cloth. The price of that performance is brittleness at the edges and a fabrication job that rewards experience, so spend your effort on choosing the fabricator, the edge profile and the cutout details rather than on the slab alone.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Once it is installed, keep it simple. Wipe with a mild stone-safe cleaner, buff dry, and keep stronger products for the rare stubborn mark. Use a flexible sealant on the wall and sink joints, leave large cuts to professionals, and read your manufacturer care guide before trying anything new. Done that way, the surface should look the same in ten years as it did on day one.
          </p>
        </div>

